    <p class="ddl-label">Language specification</p>
    <h1>Data Definition Language</h1>
    <p class="ddl-lead">Write structured data using six composable value types.</p>

    <h2 id="introduction">Overview</h2>
    <p>The Arcadia Data Definition Language (DDL) describes structured data for exchange between humans and machines.
    A document is a sequence of Unicode code points describing one value: a Boolean, Number, String, Void, List or Map.
    Lists and maps can contain other values, including nested collections.</p>
    <p>Start with the complete example and type reference below. The three translation sections then define the formal rules.
    <a href="@{siteAddress}/specifications/data-definition-language-schema/index.html">Data Definition Language Schema (DDLS)</a>
    describes constraints on DDL data; it is separate from the DDL grammar. This page does not claim that proposed DDLS versions are implemented.</p>
    <p class="box">DDL selects specific rules from the shared lexical specification.
    The <a href="#conformance">conformance notes</a> summarize these selections, verified reader boundaries and migration guidance.</p>

    @{include("./reader-guide.i")}

    <h2 id="translation">Translation pipeline</h2>
    <ol>
      <li><a href="#lexical-translation">Lexical translation</a>: Unicode code points become words (tokens).</li>
      <li><a href="#syntactical-translation">Syntactical translation</a>: whitespace, newlines and comments are discarded;
      the remaining words form one sentence.</li>
      <li><a href="#semantical-translation">Semantical translation</a>: the sentence denotes a typed value;
      duplicate names in a map are rejected.</li>
    </ol>

    @{include("./lexical-translation.i")}
    @{include("./syntactical-translation.i")}
    @{include("./semantical-translation.i")}
    @{include("./conformance.i")}

    <h2 id="references">References</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="@{siteAddress}/specifications/common-lexical-translations/index.html">Common Lexical Translations</a>: shared word productions (see DDL-specific conformance notes).</li>
      <li><a href="@{siteAddress}/specifications/mathematical-definitions-notations-and-proofs/index.html">Mathematical Definitions, Notations and Proofs</a>: grammar and list notation.</li>
      <li><a href="@{siteAddress}/specifications/data-definition-language-schema/index.html">DDLS specification</a>: schema constraints.</li>
      <li>Aho, Sethi and Ullman, <cite>Compilers: Principles, Techniques, and Tools</cite>, first edition, p. 305: syntax-directed translation.</li>
    </ul>

